{[show S]West opens the bidding and North doubles for takeout; when East raises opener's major, South can answer with a responsive double, describing the whole hand in one call rather than guessing a suit. Partner's double is for takeout: it shows opening values, shortness in \S, and support for the three unbid suits. North is asking you to name a landing spot rather than defend a low spade contract.
[BID X] ⟦With the opponents raising \S, you double too — a responsive double. Over West's major it promises the other major, four \H, together with three or more in each minor and roughly 8 to 11 points. One call tells partner you have real values but no single suit worth insisting on.⟧ Partner picks a minor, choosing \C as the safest home for the partnership's combined values. North is answering your responsive double, not showing extra strength.
[BID 4H] ⟦The opponents bounce back to \S and you carry on to your heart game. This is an aggressive push — with the known fit and competitive values you take the optimistic view, though passing 3\S and defending is the quieter, equally defensible choice.⟧
[show NESW]South's singleton \ST, four \H and 4-4 in the minors were a textbook responsive double, and North's 3\C gave the hand a home. The leap to 4\H was a bold game push; on this layout defending 3\S would have scored better. A fine reminder that the responsive double gets the partnership into the auction, but the final level is still a judgment call.}
